'''angelology''' (ān-jel-ol′ọ̄-ji), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἄγγελος, angel, + -λογία, < λέγειν, speak: see ''-ology''.] The doctrine of angels; that portion of theology which treats of angelic beings; a discourse on angels.
{{smaller block|The magic of the Moslem world is in part adopted from Jewish ''angelology'' and demonology.
{{right|''E. B. Tylor'', Encyc. Brit., XV. 203.}}
The same vast mythology commanded the general consent; the same ''angelology'', demonology.
{{right|''Milman'', Latin Christianity, xiv.}}
There was an ''angelology'', and a worship of angels, on which the Apostle animadverts with severity.
{{right|''G. P. Fisher'', Begin. of Christianity, xi.}}}}

'''angelophany''' (ān-jel-of′ạ-ni), ''n.''; pl. ''angelophanies'' (-niz). [< Gr. ἄγγελος, angel, + -φανία, < φαίνειν, show, φαίνεσθαι, appear. Cf. ''theophany'', ''epiphany''.] The visible manifestation of an angel or angels to man.
{{smaller block|If God seeks to commune more fully with a man, his messenger appears and speaks to him. The narratives of such ''angelophanies'' vary in detail. {{right|''Prof. W. R. Smith.''}}}}

'''angelophone''' (ān′jel-ọ̄-fōn), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἄγγελος, angel, + φωνή, voice.] The harmonium or parlor-organ. [Eng.; rare.]

'''angelot''' (an′je-lọt; F. pron. aṅzh′lō), ''n.'' [< OF. ''angelot'', a young or little angel (= Sp. ''angelote''), dim. of ''angele'', < LL. ''angelus'', angel. Cf. ''angelet'', with diff. dim. suffix, and see ''angel'', 5.] '''1.''' The name of a French gold coin, weighing from 97.22 to 87.96 grains, first issued in 1340 by Philip VI. {{smaller|On its obverse is an angel (whence the name of the coin) holding a cross and shield; on its reverse a cross, ornamented.}}
